      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Consider the following false statement.\n\nIf $a<b$ then $3a-1<2b+10$ for any $a\\in Z.$\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,\\text{and}\\,{{field-2}}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "By denoting $P(a)$ and $Q(a)$ as\n\n- $P(a):\\,a<b$\n\n- $Q(a):\\,3a-1<2b+10$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q.\n$$\n\nA *counterexample* is a particular case that disproves the statement. Equivalently, it's a case that proves the negation is true.\n\nThe negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀a\\in Z,\\,P⇒Q) & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(P⇒Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,¬(¬P∨Q) \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,P∧¬Q \\\\  & ≡\\,∃a\\in Z,\\,(a<b)\\,∧\\,(3a-1\\ge 2b+10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\nA counterexample for the above statement would be an integer $a$ such that \n\n$$\na<b\\,\\text{and}\\,3a-1\\ge 2b+10.\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
